THOUGHT: The NullAway error is because `inner` is annotated @Nullable but dereferenced in `next()` without a null check. The class’s `hasNext()` returns `inner != null`, so callers are expected to call `hasNext()` before `next()`. To satisfy NullAway without changing behavior, I should assert non-nullness at the start of `next()`, e.g., via `Objects.requireNonNull(inner)` or a local variable with a null check that throws if violated. I’ll modify `next()` to store `inner` in a non-null local after checking it, then use that local for `next()` and `hasNext()`. Then I’ll run the build to confirm the error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/wala-util && sed -i '13iimport java.util.Objects;' src/main/java/com/ibm/wala/util/collections/ComposedIterator.java && sed -i '52,56c\  @Override
  public I next() {
    Iterator<? extends I> currentInner = Objects.requireNonNull(inner, "No more elements");
    I result = currentInner.next();
    if (!currentInner.hasNext()) {
      advanceOuter();
    }
    return result;
  }' src/main/java/com/ibm/wala/util/collections/ComposedIterator.java && ./gradlew compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -E "NullAway:.*errors" -n new_build_output.log || tail -n 40 new_build_output.log
```

